Unix utility 'raava'

The Unix utility 'raava' shows shells in windows. It takes a virtual terminal of its own,
the first one from tty2 to tty7 with no process on it, so the clock and the logins stay where
they are, and goes back to the one it was called from when it ends. Each window is a shell on
a pseudoterminal, and what the shell draws stays in its window, in the size the window has.

The windows overlap, the one in focus on top with its border in the colors of the theme
swapped. Keys go to the window in focus, Ctrl+C included, which ends only what runs in it.
Ctrl+A starts a command of 'raava', followed by one of these letters:

c - Opens a window
n - Brings the window at the bottom to the top, so repeating it goes through them all
x - Closes the window in focus, and ends its shell
r - Draws the whole screen again
q - Closes every window and leaves
h, j, k, l - Move the window in focus left, down, up and right
H, J, K, L - Make it narrower, taller, shorter and wider
Ctrl+A - Sends Ctrl+A itself to the window

With the mouse, a click brings a window to the top, dragging its title moves it and dragging
its bottom right corner changes its size. A window whose shell exits closes by itself, and
'raava' leaves once no window is left.

A program already running in a window sees a new size only the next time it asks for it.
The virtual terminal of 'raava' is held for it alone, so a program in a window that draws on
the console directly, instead of writing to its terminal, draws nothing there. What it writes
to its terminal still shows in its window, and Ctrl+C ends it as any other.

USAGE:

raava

For more information, enter 'raava ?' or 'raava --help'.

